          %h2#references References
          %p The terminology “bridge” is first used in this sense in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Nuyts"}> Andreas Nuyts
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrea+Vezzosi"} Andrea Vezzosi
              = succeed "," do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Dominique Devriese
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Dominique+Devriese"} ?
              = succeed "," do
                %em Parametric quantifiers for dependent type theory
              %strong 1
              (2017) 1-29 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1145/3110276"}> doi:10.1145/3110276
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Bridge types first appear in an unary form, written as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⟦
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⟧
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in \llbracket A \rrbracket
            rather than
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Br
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Br}_A(x)
          %ul
            %li#BM12
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Philippe+Bernardy"} Jean-Philippe Bernardy
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Guilhem Moulin
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Guilhem+Moulin"} ?
              %em A Computational Interpretation of Parametricity.
              In Proceedings of the 27th Annual IEEE Symposium on Logic in Computer Science, LICS 2012, Dubrovnik, Croatia, June 25-28, 2012. IEEE Computer Society, 135–144. &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1109/LICS.2012.25"}> doi:10.1109/LICS.2012.25
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p An equivalent of univalence for unary bridge types appears (with inverse conditions given by the Pair-Pred and Surj-Typ equations) in
          %ul
            %li#BCM15
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Philippe+Bernardy"}> Jean-Philippe Bernardy
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thierry+Coquand"} Thierry Coquand
              = succeed "." do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Guilhem Moulin
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Guilhem+Moulin"} ?
              %em A Presheaf Model of Parametric Type Theory.
              In The 31st Conference on the Mathematical Foundations of Programming Semantics, MFPS 2015, Nijmegen, The Netherlands, June 22-25, 2015 (Electronic Notes in Theoretical Computer Science, Vol. 319), Dan R. Ghica (Ed.). Elsevier, 67–82. &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.entcs.2015.12.006"}> doi:10.1016/j.entcs.2015.12.006
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p The equivalent of univalence for (binary) bridge types is called “relativity” in:
          %ul
            %li#CH21
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Evan+Cavallo"} Evan Cavallo
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Harper"} Robert Harper
              %em Internal Parametricity for Cubical Type Theory.
              Log. Methods Comput. Sci. 17, 4 (2021). &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.46298/lmcs-17(4:5)2021"}> doi:10.46298/lmcs-17(4:5)2021
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11290"} arXiv:2005.11290
          %p
            A unary bridge type called “display” and written as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^d(x)
            instead of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Br
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Br}_A(x)
            appears in
          %ul
            %li#KS23
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Astra+Kolomatskaia"}> Astra Kolomatskaia
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Shulman"} Michael Shulman
              %em Displayed Type Theory and Semi-Simplicial Types
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.18781"}> arXiv:2311.18781
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p A fibered version of bridge types is defined in:
          %ul
            %li#ACKS24
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thorsten+Altenkirch"}> Thorsten Altenkirch
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yorgo+Chamoun"} Yorgo Chamoun
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ambrus+Kaposi"} Ambrus Kaposi
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Shulman"} Michael Shulman
              = succeed "," do
                %em Internal parametricity, without an interval
              %strong 8
              (2024) 2340-2369 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.06448"}> arXiv:2307.06448
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1145/3632920"} doi:10.1145/3632920
          %p
            Bridge types in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Narya"} Narya
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em> Observational higher dimensions
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Narya"} Narya
                documentation. (
                %a{:href => "https://narya.readthedocs.io/en/latest/observational.html"}> web
                )
            %li
              %p
                %em> Parametricity
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Narya"} Narya
                documentation. (
                %a{:href => "https://narya.readthedocs.io/en/latest/parametricity.html"}> web
                )
          %p
            A blog post from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topos+Institute"} Topos Institute
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bridge+types"} bridge types
            for the theory of version control:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Jaz+Myers"}> David Jaz Myers
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Structure-aware version control via observational bridge types
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topos+Institute"} Topos Institute
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://topos.institute/blog/2024-11-13-structure-aware-version-control-via-observational-bridge-types/"} web
          %p Some discussion of bridge types occurs in
          %ul
            %li#CTZulip
              %em> synthetic algebraic geometry vs continuation passing style
              , Category Theory Zulip,
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://categorytheory.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/229199-learning.3A-questions/topic/synthetic.20algebraic.20geometry.20vs.20continuation.20passing.20style/with/526838004"} web
